      <p>Start with the base <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Carcassonne+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Carcassonne</a>. I found it at $31.99 at Target, Walmart, Game Nerdz and Miniature Market the week I checked, against a $39.99 list price. Under $35 is normal, and under $32 is a good day. Every rule in this article refers to that box. Full stats live on my <a href="/game/carcassonne">Carcassonne page</a>.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. At five players the map gets thin, the tile draw starts to matter more than your decisions, and a game can drag to 50 minutes. It is a 2 to 4 player game wearing a 2 to 5 label.</p>

      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Carcassonne+Inns+and+Cathedrals+expansion&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Inns &amp; Cathedrals</a> is the first expansion I would add, and it is cheap for how much it changes: I saw it between $15 and $20. It brings the big follower, worth two followers for majority purposes, plus inns that double the value of a road and a cathedral that doubles the value of a city. It also adds a sixth player. That one box fixes the five player warning above, mostly.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. The big follower makes majority fights nastier, and a cathedral that never gets finished is a dead tile. Expect one argument per game.</p>

      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Carcassonne+Hunters+and+Gatherers+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Hunters and Gatherers</a> is a stand-alone game, not an expansion, and its tiles do not mix with the base box. Same engine, Stone Age setting: hunters lie down in the meadows the way farmers lie down in the original, gatherers stand in the forests, fishers stand on the rivers, and huts score off the fish in a river system instead of cloisters. Each player gets 5 tribe members and 2 huts instead of 7 followers, which makes the follower shortage sharper, and the box has 79 tiles with a volcano as the starting tile. It runs about $32, and I have seen it near $32 at gift and hobby shops. Its community rating sits lower than the original, 7.1 against 7.4, and for good reason: it is the same game with a new skin. Buy it after 50 plays of the base box, not before. Stats are on my <a href="/game/carcassonne-hunters-and-gatherers">Hunters and Gatherers page</a>.</p>

      <p>There is also a Big Box that bundles the base game with several expansions. Skip it on your first purchase. It costs more than the base game plus the one expansion you actually need, and half of it will sit sealed on the shelf for a year. That was my $70 lesson.</p>

      <h2>The Shelf Test</h2>
      <p>Here is the Shelf Test. Would this game survive 10 plays on a shelf that already holds 40 boxes? Carcassonne passes, and it passes for a reason I did not expect: the rules stop being the game after night one. Ten plays in, you are not asking where the tile goes. You are asking whether you can afford another follower, and that question has not gotten old in five years.</p>

      <p>The flaw is real, though. Farm counting at the end is fiddly, and you will have one argument per game about which field touches which city. If your group hates end-game math, this is the wrong tile game for you. I have had that argument at least 20 times and I still bring the box out.</p>

      <p>If you want a game that plays cleaner at two players, my <a href="/blog/best-2-player-board-games">two player list</a> covers the ones that hold up. If you want the wider field of gateway games that live next to Carcassonne on my shelf, the <a href="/blog/best-strategy-board-games">strategy picks</a> and the <a href="/blog/best-board-games-for-beginners">beginner list</a> are where I would start, and the full <a href="/games/strategy">strategy lineup</a> covers what to buy after this.</p>

      <p class="cta">Here is the part that matters. Get the base <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Carcassonne+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Carcassonne</a> first, play it knowing that the last two minutes of counting decide the game, and keep one follower in reserve for the fields. It was $31.99 the week I checked, list price $39.99, so under $32 is a solid deal. If your group already plays it twice a week, add <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Carcassonne+Inns+and+Cathedrals+expansion&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Inns &amp; Cathedrals</a> for $15 to $20 before you buy any other box. Let my wallet take the hit on the Big Box instead of yours.</p>

